 ## Background
-The concept of composite training objectives in volume rendering emerged with the rise of Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) and subsequent differentiable rendering techniques. Early works like NeRF utilized a combination of photometric loss (L1/SSIM) and regularization terms to stabilize training . As the field evolved, specialized losses were introduced to address specific challenges: Total Variation (TV) for smoothness in voxel-based methods [S1], semantic consistency for open-vocabulary NeRFs , and geometric constraints (Eikonal, depth) for implicit surface representations [S1]. The joint optimization of pose and geometry, as seen in dynamic scene reconstruction, further expanded the complexity of these composite objectives, requiring dynamic weighting strategies to balance conflicting gradients [S1].
+The concept of composite training objectives in neural rendering emerged alongside the rise of Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) and subsequent differentiable volume rendering techniques. Early works like [S1] (Ray-Distance Volume Rendering for Neural Scene Reconstruction, 2024) formalized the aggregation of geometric (depth, normal, Eikonal) and photometric losses to stabilize training and improve geometry quality. Concurrently, Gaussian Splatting variants introduced codebook and masking losses to handle explicit representations [10621-overall-training-loss.md]. The trend has moved towards multi-stage and multi-scale objectives to handle dynamic scene growth and detail refinement [13064-multi-scale-joint-training-loss.md], as well as joint optimization with auxiliary networks for deformation and semantics [16752-joint-training-losses.md].
 
